\begin{abstract}
The widespread adoption of edge‑computing devices has created an urgent demand for neural network inference that operates within tight energy and latency budgets. Existing quantization and model‑compression methods either sacrifice predictive accuracy or require extensive hardware support, limiting their practicality on resource‑constrained platforms. To address these shortcomings, we propose a hardware‑aware mixed‑precision quantization framework that jointly determines optimal layer‑wise bit‑widths and scaling factors through a differentiable search process guided by a lightweight reinforcement‑learning controller. This controller adapts precision configurations to satisfy predefined latency constraints while preserving model performance, thereby uniting automated architecture search with hardware‑centric optimization. Empirical evaluation on large‑scale vision benchmarks demonstrates up to a 3.2‑fold reduction in inference energy consumption and a 2.1‑times speedup in execution time, with less than 1 % degradation in top‑1 accuracy relative to full‑precision baselines. The results confirm that the proposed approach enables deployment of state‑of‑the‑art deep learning models on micro‑controllers and other low‑power devices, expanding the scope of on‑device artificial intelligence in real‑world applications.
\end{abstract}